shown here instead of  the check on deployment next we will  configure the source from virtual  machines that are going to be migrated  and you can configure multiple sources  in this demo we will define the VMware  on Prem as a source so we'll configure  the manager address for migrate for  compute engine and the cloud extension  to which we want to connect to so that  we can use Maya for compute engine as a  transport and you can see the source as  configured next we'll create a migration  plan for this will identify the VM we  would like to migrate this is the VM we  saw on the VM side and since it is a  stateless application we will specify an  image for the intent flag and then  create the migration command to ask only  for image extraction the migration plan  is written into a Yama file  can be reviewed and customized  in the ammo file you'll see the  definitions for the different artifacts  that are going to be generated as well  as default filters for exclusions of  directories and files that are not going  to be included in the image as well as  the target image names for deploying  into GCR once these are built next we're  going to generate the artifacts  basically feeding this migration plan  into the migrant for anthos will execute  the migration and the breakdown of these  different artifacts for this we will use  the make CTL generate artifacts command  first the migration plan is validated  and then it's applied to the cluster the  migration is applied as a C R D so you  can also use the CR D as an API to  automate this migration then you'll use  the make CTL migration status to monitor  the status and progress of this  migration throughout it's different  activities  what you'll see now is that since we are  communicating with the on-prem VMware  system the VMS that are still running on  Prem will be picked into a snapshot and  from that we will start extracting the  image here we can see that the image  extraction applied into GCR with the  image name that we configured into the  migration plan so we skipped the entire  upload process to GCR  in addition in a bucket in GCS you will  find the set of crated artifacts as  dockerfile and deployment artifacts that  are usable for further deployment and  day to operations when you'd like to  update this image later to download the  artifacts you can use the mix ETL  migration get - artifacts command with  the migration name and you can see the  deployment spec that was automatically  created or the image that was  automatically built and this deployments  pack can be applied to this or any other  cluster where you would like to run  these workloads so let's go ahead and  apply the deployment so that we can see  the application running  you  now that the deployment is applied it is  inserted into the environment we pre  created just as a reminder we have a  load balancer setup and we also have an  auto scaler configured so when this  deployment goes into place the  autoscaler kicks in automatically and  sets up a minimum and maximum pods and  the monitoring on the CPU metrics  according to those conditions so you can  immediately see some of the kubernetes  capabilities in action  now let's connect to the load balancer  and you can see the application running  will just connect to this internal load  balancer and you can see the application  now running in G ke  you  now that the application is running  we're switching to talk about day 2  operations let's say that we have a  package that is a user-mode package in  the image and we would like to update it  in this case we will put the user-mode  package for update in the doctor file  that we downloaded as part of the  generate artifacts this is for example  an open SSH package then this doctor  file can be fed into cloud build with a  single g-cloud command and we get a new  built image that includes the updated  package we're using here an update for  SUSE a Linux so we're using the package  manager in zipper to update the image  we want you to see the image built we've  accelerated this process  it usually takes 12 to 13 minutes to  complete but we sped this up a bit for  the sake of this demo  you  now that the image is updated and built  if we go to the repository we can see  the updated image version and we can go  and perform a rolling update on the  actual deployment remember that we have  now a deployment with three pods running  the web front-end so we're going to  apply a live update in this live update  you will see the new version being  provisioned alongside the old version  and then the old version being  decommissioned during this process the  application is staying up  and you can see that here while this  process is going on the application is  still accessible and in a few seconds  the update process will be completed  you  and now we can see the updated image  version has been applied and we did not  have to touch any of these instances  individually  that concludes our demo of my great for  anthos today thank you so much for  taking the time to watch it and we look  forward to more demo videos in the  future
 

